Z||000004||Tryxalon||05-01-2001||07:07 PM||||I'm not quite as thrilled about your situation as I would expect. <P>What you don't say: What motivation does he have for the car?  <BR>Does he want the car or do you want him to want the car?<P>We all know Fieros are more than just a car - they are actually a "relationship" ... and if he is not ga-ga over the car he will not appreciate it, won't take care of it, and will abuse your largesse. <P>Sounds to me like you're trying to teach some responsibility ... good goal but is a car (especially one as "intensive" as a Fiero) the tool to teach with?  Perhaps some smaller steps first?? <P>But it is your choice... Just my 2 cents. <P> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Philphine||05-01-2001||08:10 PM||||thats kind of one of my concerns. he has expressed no interest, but at this point i haven't talked to him to even know if he is. like i said, i'm not from a very well to do family. that may be a bit of an understatement. i don't think he is even thinking in terms of even owning a car at this time, period.<P>i would oviously talk to him about weather or not he wants the car and not just have it in his driveway with a bow on it. i just can't predict for myself how responsible he'll be even if he is really enthusiastic about it. <P>i do mean to offer it to him though (barring his mothers objection, which i can't see her doing). i'll just have to hope i don't regret it. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||TWEETIE BIRD||05-01-2001||08:45 PM||||Just my opinion; I don't think it would be fair to the others. It will make them resentful unless you do the same for them.<BR>Make a deal or barter with him if he wants it he can earn it the hard way. Giving it to him<BR>will not make him appreciate it. Just my 2cents worth. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Nashco||05-01-2001||11:31 PM||||I can relate with this a lot, and I think how I did it worked very very well.  I am now 19, but when I turned 15 this was the scenario:  My cousin had a convertible baja bug that she was tired of owning because it required repair all the time.  My aunt (the owner) offered me the car for 2 grand, about 500 less than it was worth on the market.  I would take up payments for hardly anything a month, 55 bucks.  This put me into payments for three years (not so coincidentally when I was to graduate), but the payments were easy to make if I was short on cash.<P>Here's the kicker; my dad thought it would be a great learning tool for me, and it was.  I didn't know jack about vw's then, and hadn't ever considered them before.  I agreed to the deal on the car because it was convertible and orange, that was it.  Well, I fell in love with it.  It needed something every single weekend, and just like our fieros, almost always something cheap for parts but required some time.  This kept me busy and out of trouble, and I slowly grew prouder and more respectful of the car because *I* was the one putting time and money into it.  I really really got into the VW scene, and I still am.  I started off with a bone stock 1500, and as I could afford stuff, I would improve it.  It was great because the car started off slow, easy to control, and still cool.  I eventually had a 1600 with a weber carb and mallory distributor, among many small things.  The car is very quick for a stock VW, but now I'm deep into it.  I have a 4.3 chevy v6 and porsche 4 speed tranny that I'm working on getting in there.<P>Well, probably more info than you wanted, but I have grown A HUGE respect for cars and desire to learn everything I can about them.  When I was 16 I wanted to be a computer programmer, now I am going to college to be an automotive engineer.<P>Just because somebody doesn't show interest now doesn't mean they won't.  With the right nurturing and guidance, a boy can fall in love with his cars <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  My advice would be to agree with him he can have it if he comes to your place and helps you every step of the way to get it driveable.  Setting the standard of proper maintenance and attention to detail can really help a new driver.  You could also make some sort of plan where he has to either pay you a minimal amount, or help on your cars once a month or something (just an idea).  My dad was a mechanic and I learned a ton from him, without his help and instructions, I wouldn't have known where to go or have any basic knowledge.<P>Good luck with the car.  I love teaching people new stuff if they're interested, as most people do.  If you like it, and you get the kid interested, who knows?  Maybe he'll like cars just as much as you soon...<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||DRH||05-01-2001||11:56 PM||||Because of the younger kids, I'd lean more toward selling it to him... selling him a car you don't need any more doesn't obligate you to do the same for them... might help ensure his resposibility too if he's got something in it. Give him a good deal. Maybe a payment plan... barter maybe... mow your yard x number of times???<P>Don't get your hopes too high... Most kids have to fail a few times before they learn responsibility... even if he trashes it, maybe it'll help him learn not to do it next time.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Bob Englert||05-02-2001||12:19 AM||||My daughter needed a car to drive to school when she got her license.  We found a 87 Coupe that was getting close to being retired to the boneyard (158K, rod knock, etc).  We had Ed Parks put a Grooms remanufactured Duke in it and had it painted when she was at summer school at Duke last summer.<P>The deal I made with Megan was that if she qualified to attend the academic awards banquet at her high school all 4 years, then I would give her the car when she graduated from High school.  When we made the deal we didn't know it would be a Fiero, but she has grown to really love the car and understands how unique and safe they are.  <P>I am proud to say that she really drives the car with a lot of care and wants to keep it for a long time.  She has to help with maintenance and repairs and I am pounding the need to check oil, air pressure, etc.  and to what the temp gauge at times.  I am truly amazed when she spends 1/2 day on the weekend to wash and polish the car.<P>The Fiero has proven to be an effective incentive for my daughter.  She just made the banquet for her junior year, so 1 more to go (she is 3rd in her class, had to brag a little).  Maybe you can arrange to let the person drive the car and maintain insurance but not actually give him the car until graduation or maintaining a certain GPA or something.  Whatever is important to you and the family.<P>Just a thought...<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
